+/***********************************************************************
+ *           emulate_skipped_cpuid
+ *
+ * ROSETTA_SMC_CPUID: Rosetta 2 runs the instructions of a block as it translated
+ * them, even when an instruction of the block has just rewritten the next one. On
+ * x86 the rewritten instruction runs. Copy protection decrypts a cpuid that way
+ * (add dword [rip+0], key; then the four bytes it changed): Rosetta runs the old
+ * bytes and, when the old first instruction is one byte long, carries on from the
+ * cpuid's second byte, 0xa2, which decodes as mov [imm64], al and faults on the
+ * eight bytes after it. That fault, at RIP, with 0f a2 at RIP - 1 and the address
+ * those eight bytes hold, means the cpuid never ran: it is run here and the thread
+ * resumes after it. A cpuid skipped without a fault cannot be seen from here.
